# Chronicle self-host configuration. `./chronicle setup` writes `.env` from this file; to
# edit by hand, copy it to `.env`, fill it in, then run:
#
#   ./chronicle up
#
# Every value the stack needs is in this one file.
# Generate every secret with:  openssl rand -base64 32.  Never commit `.env`.

# ---- Public hostname -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The FQDN that participants' phones and researchers' browsers use (e.g. study.example.org).
DOMAIN=chronicle.example.org
# Dedicated Chronicle bridge subnet for the edge network, which carries only the web proxy's
# published ports. Use an unused private IPv4 network if this overlaps the host.
CHRONICLE_SUBNET=172.28.0.0/16
# First three octets of the internal /24 that carries web, backend, PostgreSQL and backups.
# setup and update write a free one (network-subnet.py select-core); web holds .2 and the backend
# trusts forwarded client addresses from that address only. Change it only if the range collides
# with a route here; Compose refuses to start without it.
CHRONICLE_CORE_PREFIX=
# Automatic operator reconciliation. A saved false is preserved by upgrade and adoption.
# Setup writes false on a host without Linux cron; nothing refuses to run without it.
CHRONICLE_RECONCILE_ENABLED=true
# Exact upstream proxy ranges. A same-host proxy reaches Caddy through the Docker gateway.
# For a remote F5, exact source CIDRs enable forwarded client identity; client XFF is optional.
CADDY_TRUSTED_PROXIES=172.28.0.1/32
# TCP sources permitted to the LB pool listener. Blank accepts any source. A host firewall
# (UFW, firewalld) does not filter Docker's published ports, which Docker forwards before those
# input rules run; set the load balancer CIDR(s) here, or filter in the DOCKER-USER chain
# (README "Host firewalls and published ports").
POOL_ALLOWED_IPS=
PUBLIC_HTTP_ENABLED=false
DASHBOARD_BASE_URL=
START_WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=3600
# Health wait for the new release during ./chronicle update. Unset means START_WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS.
#UPGRADE_WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=
POSTGRES_MAX_SLOT_WAL_KEEP_SIZE=4GB
# Optional only when the participant-facing HTTPS origin differs from https://DOMAIN.
# It must be a root origin (no path/query/fragment). Blank deterministically uses https://DOMAIN.
# Production modes accept public DNS names or globally routable IP literals only; private,
# loopback, documentation, benchmarking, multicast, and broadcast literals fail closed.
CHRONICLE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=
# Trial mode only: setup prefers standard HTTPS/CA ports and selects free alternatives
# when another host service already owns them. The app URL includes a nonstandard port.
LOCAL_HTTPS_PORT=443
LOCAL_HTTP_PORT=80

# ---- What gets deployed ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Docker Compose reads both of these from this file, so plain `docker compose up -d` picks
# them up with no -f arguments and no wrapper script.
#
# Keep this distinct from every other Compose deployment on the host. Compose keys volumes
# off the project name, so reusing an existing name could adopt another deployment's data
# volumes without its encryption keyring.
COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=chronicle-selfhost
# Mutable operator state is separate from release files. Fresh installs keep it beside this
# Compose file; `./chronicle update` rewrites this to the absolute previous
# state location so backups and TLS material survive versioned bundle directories.
CHRONICLE_STATE_DIR=.
#
# COMPOSE_FILE is the deployment. Exactly one overlays/mode-*.yml must be in it -- it
# decides which Caddy config is mounted and which ports are published, together, so they
# cannot disagree:
#
#   mode-behind-proxy-internal.yml   your load balancer terminates TLS; the dashboard is
#                                    served only on INTERNAL_BIND, while phones still
#                                    upload from the internet          (RECOMMENDED)
#   mode-own-tls-internal.yml        this stack terminates TLS with cert.pem/key.pem that
#                                    you put in ./tls; private dashboard
#   mode-local-https.yml             TRIAL ONLY: no domain name and no certificate --
#                                    Caddy runs its own CA and issues a certificate for
#                                    this machine's LAN address, which each test phone
#                                    installs once. Set DOMAIN to that address, e.g.
#                                    DOMAIN=192.168.1.50. Never for participants.
#
# Leave overlays/backups.yml in. Backups are not just a safety net here: the plain-SQL
# dumps they write are the only copy of the data that restores WITHOUT the encryption key,
# so removing them while ENABLE_ENCRYPTION=true is refused before anything starts.
#
# Add overlays/monitoring.yml for Grafana.
# The exact supported combinations are listed in docs/DEPLOYMENT-COMPATIBILITY.md.
COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:overlays/mode-behind-proxy-internal.yml:overlays/backups.yml

# ---- Encryption at rest ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Encrypts the database files with Percona pg_tde (already in POSTGRES_IMAGE — nothing
# extra to install). The key is generated for you on first start and kept on its own
# Docker volume, separate from the data, so a copy of the data volume alone is useless.
#
# Protects: a stolen disk, or a copied postgres_data volume.
# Does not protect: anyone with root on this running box — the key is on the box, which is
# the trade for not having a passphrase you could lose.
#
# If you lose the keyring the data volume is unrecoverable. Two things stop that being
# fatal, and both are automatic: ./backups holds plain-SQL dumps that need no key at all,
# and a copy of the keyring is placed in ./backups/keyring. Back up ./backups as one unit
# and you can always get back in. `./chronicle verify` checks both are present.
#
# Turning this off also means removing overlays/backups.yml is allowed; leaving it on
# without that overlay is refused before anything starts.
ENABLE_ENCRYPTION=true

# ---- Rate limiting (per client IP) -----------------------------------------------------
# Aggregate abuse ceilings; the backend applies authenticated device/session and per-route
# limits. A trusted SNAT LB without client XFF has a fixed 20,000/mobile-window fleet ceiling.
# Raise the per-client ceilings only after measuring legitimate bursts.
RATE_LIMIT_MOBILE_ABUSE_EVENTS=200
LB_MAX_BODY_BYTES=67108864
LB_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=300
LB_MAX_HEADER_BYTES=16384
RATE_LIMIT_MOBILE_WINDOW=1s
# Pause (seconds) between ./chronicle verify's rate-limit probes. Unset means 1.2.
#RATE_LIMIT_RETRY_SLEEP=
RATE_LIMIT_WEB_ABUSE_EVENTS=200
RATE_LIMIT_WEB_WINDOW=1s
# Internal dashboard listener: every request per peer, ahead of the password check, so
# password guessing is bounded on every path; dashboard login also has app throttling.
RATE_LIMIT_GUARD_EVENTS=300
RATE_LIMIT_GUARD_WINDOW=1m

# ---- Where the listeners bind ----------------------------------------------------------
# In the behind-proxy modes this is the pool port your load balancer forwards to.
# Keep HTTP_BIND=127.0.0.1 if that proxy runs on this same machine; set it to a private
# address the proxy can reach if it does not. Nothing on this box can detect the mistake --
# an off-host balancer simply gets connection refused while everything here looks healthy.
# Make sure the proxy sends `X-Forwarded-Proto: https`; Caddy here honors it. Both binds take
# IPv4 addresses only: the stack network is IPv4, so an IPv6 listener would hide client addresses.
HTTP_BIND=127.0.0.1
HTTP_PORT=8080
# Off loopback the pool listener must run TLS (setup sets true): it serves the internal
# certificate from ./tls, and the load balancer trusts ./tls/internal-ca.crt. Plain HTTP off
# loopback needs a controlled-register reference to an approved waiver instead.
POOL_LISTENER_TLS=true
#POOL_PLAINTEXT_WAIVER=

# The own-tls modes publish these instead. Port 80 serves only the redirect to 443.
HTTPS_PORT=443
TLS_HTTP_PORT=80

# The internal-dashboard modes add this listener. Bind it to a VPN/management interface,
# e.g. INTERNAL_BIND=10.0.0.5. Leaving it at 127.0.0.1 exposes it to no network at all --
# reach it over an SSH tunnel. It runs TLS with a self-signed certificate generated on
# first start; extra names for that certificate go in INTERNAL_CERT_SANS, in openssl SAN
# syntax, e.g. INTERNAL_CERT_SANS='DNS:chronicle.internal,IP:10.0.0.9'.
INTERNAL_BIND=127.0.0.1
INTERNAL_PORT=8081
INTERNAL_CERT_SANS=

# DASHBOARD_ORIGINS: extra browser origins allowed to call the API, space-separated and
# including the scheme, e.g. 'https://chronicle.internal:8081 https://10.0.0.9:8081'.
# https://$DOMAIN and the internal listener as https://127.0.0.1:$INTERNAL_PORT and
# https://localhost:$INTERNAL_PORT are always allowed, which covers the common cases (the
# public hostname, and an SSH tunnel to the internal listener). Add a name here if you open
# the dashboard on any OTHER host — the backend rejects an unlisted Origin, and the symptom
# is not an error page: the dashboard renders, the header shows "awaiting-sso", and sign-in
# never completes.
DASHBOARD_ORIGINS=

# ---- Researcher dashboard access control ------------------------------------------------
# Participant/mobile paths are never gated — phones cannot answer a password prompt.
# The internal listener is gated by BOTH a source-address allowlist and a single global
# dashboard password login, and it runs TLS to protect credentials and session cookies.
#
# DASHBOARD_ALLOWED_IPS: space-separated CIDRs allowed to reach the dashboard at all.
#   Matched against the real TCP peer address, so it cannot be forged with a header.
#   Host access and SSH tunnels use the Compose gateway; add approved remote sources.
DASHBOARD_ALLOWED_IPS='172.28.0.1/32 127.0.0.1/32 ::1/128'
# bcrypt hash ONLY -- never the password itself. `./chronicle setup` reads a password
# silently and sends it to the digest-pinned Caddy image over stdin. The manual no-argv
# procedure is documented in README.md under "Editing .env by hand instead".
# Single-quote it: a bcrypt hash contains $ signs that would otherwise be expanded.
DASHBOARD_PASSWORD_HASH=

# ---- Database ------------------------------------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_USER=chronicle
POSTGRES_DB=chronicle
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME_strong_db_password
POSTGRES_APP_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME_independent_runtime_db_password

# ---- Dashboard session signing --------------------------------------------------------
# Signs dashboard session tokens (HS256). MUST be at least 32 characters -- the JWT decoder
# rejects a shorter key with "The secret length must be at least 256 bits", and the symptom
# is a dashboard that loads and then fails every login with HTTP 500, not a startup error.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
JWT_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_jwt_secret_min_32_characters
# Setup-owned generation dates only (JSON); preserved through re-setup and upgrades.
CHRONICLE_SECRET_GENERATED_AT=
# Minutes a dashboard login lasts before the password is asked again (default 480 = 8 h).
DASHBOARD_SESSION_MINUTES=480
# Maximum idle time before the backend asks a researcher to sign in again (1 through 120).
CHRONICLE_SESSION_IDLE_MINUTES=15

# Internal Caddy-to-backend credential. Public clients enroll for per-device keys.
CHRONICLE_INTERNAL_WEB_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_internal_web_secret_min_32_characters

# true = record a keyed fingerprint of staff (dashboard) IP addresses in the audit trail and
# logs. Participant IP addresses are never recorded.
CHRONICLE_RECORD_STAFF_IP=false

# ---- Google Play reviewer access (optional) ------------------------------------------
# The reusable Play Console credential can mint only a fresh 15-minute, one-time
# enrollment invitation for this one synthetic study/participant. It never authenticates
# dashboard/admin APIs. Leave disabled until the study is active and the synthetic participant
# explicitly has ENROLLED status; the public route never changes researcher-controlled status.
# Set the two non-secret scope values, then run `./chronicle rotate-secret reviewer` to
# generate the secret directly into this mode-0600 file and enable the route. Copy it from
# a trusted editor into Play Console; never put it in Git, a URL, logs, or support text.
CHRONICLE_REVIEWER_ACCESS_ENABLED=false
CHRONICLE_REVIEWER_ACCESS_SECRET=
CHRONICLE_REVIEWER_STUDY_ID=
CHRONICLE_REVIEWER_PARTICIPANT_ID=

# ---- Dashboard login -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shipped internal dashboards use /chronicle/v3/auth/dashboard-login with an explicit
# password, verified against DASHBOARD_PASSWORD_HASH generated by setup. Sign-out and idle
# lock require password entry again. Credentialless testing login must remain disabled.
# Upgrade/adopt/reconcile migrate older internal settings; a missing hash requires setup.
TESTING_LOGIN_ENABLED=false

# Internal password sessions have no MFA claim, so the internal modes need false (config-guard
# refuses true with the dashboard password). Public identity-provider integrations still
# require MFA and separate review.
REQUIRE_MFA=false

# ---- Metrics endpoint credentials --------------------------------------------------------
# The backend will not start unless METRICS_PASSWORD is at least 32 characters. It protects
# the /prometheus scrape endpoint, which Caddy also refuses to serve from outside.
METRICS_USERNAME=chronicle-metrics
METRICS_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME_metrics_password_min_32_chars

# ---- Image pins ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A tagged Chronicle release replaces the three placeholders below with immutable
# registry references (`name@sha256:...`). Do not replace them with `latest` or another
# mutable tag. Source-checkout developers can override them with locally built image tags.
RELEASE_VERSION=development
BACKEND_IMAGE=__CHRONICLE_BACKEND_IMAGE__
SELFHOST_FRONTEND_IMAGE=__CHRONICLE_SELFHOST_FRONTEND_IMAGE__
CADDY_IMAGE=__CHRONICLE_SELFHOST_CADDY_IMAGE__
POSTGRES_IMAGE=__CHRONICLE_SELFHOST_POSTGRES_IMAGE__

# ---- Resource limits (tune to the box) -------------------------------------------------
# Containers are capped so one runaway process cannot starve the host.
POSTGRES_MEM_LIMIT=2g
BACKEND_MEM_LIMIT=3g
WEB_MEM_LIMIT=256m

# Container logs are unbounded by default and will eventually fill the disk.
LOG_MAX_SIZE=10m
LOG_MAX_FILES=5

# ---- Postgres tuning -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Postgres' own defaults (128MB shared_buffers, a 4GB effective_cache_size that ignores
# the container limit, spinning-disk cost constants) leave most of POSTGRES_MEM_LIMIT
# unused and mis-cost index scans on SSD. These values are sized for POSTGRES_MEM_LIMIT=2g
# at roughly 25% / 75%. Raise them together with the limit, and keep
#   shared_buffers + (max_connections x work_mem) + maintenance_work_mem
# under it so the kernel never OOM-kills the database.
POSTGRES_SHARED_BUFFERS=512MB
POSTGRES_EFFECTIVE_CACHE_SIZE=1536MB
POSTGRES_WORK_MEM=8MB
POSTGRES_MAINTENANCE_WORK_MEM=128MB
POSTGRES_WAL_BUFFERS=16MB
POSTGRES_MAX_WAL_SIZE=2GB
POSTGRES_MIN_WAL_SIZE=512MB
# SSD/NVMe defaults. On spinning disks use 4 and 2.
POSTGRES_RANDOM_PAGE_COST=1.1
POSTGRES_EFFECTIVE_IO_CONCURRENCY=200
# Keep 0 while ENABLE_ENCRYPTION=true: a parallel scan over a pg_tde table crashes the cluster.
# POSTGRES_MAX_PARALLEL_WORKERS_PER_GATHER=0
# Server-side bounds in milliseconds on one statement, an idle open transaction, and a lock
# wait. Exports opt out of the statement timeout in their streaming transaction.
POSTGRES_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS=300000
POSTGRES_IDLE_IN_TRANSACTION_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
POSTGRES_LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# ---- Backend JVM (tune to the box) ---------------------------------------------------
BACKEND_XMS=-Xms512m
BACKEND_XMX=-Xmx2g

# ---- overlays/backups.yml ------------------------------------------------------------
# Any go-cron schedule: a cron line with an optional leading seconds field (0 3 * * 1-5), an
# optional TZ=<zone> prefix, @hourly, @daily, @weekly, @monthly, @yearly or @every <duration> (6h, 1h30m).
# Verification and the stale-backup alert treat a dump as overdue after the longest gap + 12 h.
BACKUP_SCHEDULE=@daily
# Retention is the operator's choice; ./chronicle setup asks for each value and Compose refuses
# to start the backup sidecar without them. A number of days/weeks/months, or all to never
# delete that tier. The backup image's own defaults are 7 days, 4 weeks and 6 months.
BACKUP_KEEP_DAYS=
BACKUP_KEEP_WEEKS=
BACKUP_KEEP_MONTHS=
# pre-upgrade-*.sql.gz and pre-restore-*.sql.gz (+ .continuity.sql.gz) safety dumps in the
# backups root are outside the rotation above; each backup run deletes those older than this
# many days (1-9999), or keeps them all with all.
PRE_OP_BACKUP_KEEP_DAYS=
BACKUP_MEM_LIMIT=512m
# A restart-policy restart does not honor Compose depends_on. The backup wrapper waits this
# long for PostgreSQL plus the migrated backend before allowing the immediate dump.
BACKUP_STARTUP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=3600
BACKUP_STARTUP_INTERVAL_SECONDS=2

# Maximum time an individual secret-rotation service reconciliation may wait for health.
# The rotation command validates 1..3600 seconds and preserves its recovery transaction on
# timeout rather than guessing whether the new or old credential is live.
SECRET_ROTATION_WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=300

# Maximum post-restore startup health wait. The database restore itself is intentionally
# not killed on a wall-clock timer mid-schema; this bound applies after it completes.
RESTORE_START_WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=3600

# Free space (GiB) that update, restore and other mutating commands require on the state,
# Docker data and database filesystems before they start, and that a backup run requires on
# the backup filesystem (a run below it fails, which raises the backup-stale alert). Setup,
# check and doctor warn when the chosen retention would not fit above it. Unset means 5.
#MIN_FREE_DISK_GIB=

# ---- overlays/monitoring.yml ---------------------------------------------------------
# Required only when overlays/monitoring.yml is present in COMPOSE_FILE.
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME_grafana_admin_password_min_32_chars
GRAFANA_BIND=127.0.0.1
GRAFANA_PORT=3000
METRICS_RETENTION=30d
LOGS_RETENTION=14d
# An alert budget, not a cap: the storage dashboard graphs metrics use against it and the
# disk alert fires above it. (Called METRICS_MAX_DISK_BYTES before 2026.11.12; update renames it.)
METRICS_DISK_BUDGET_BYTES=5368709120
# A hard cap: VictoriaLogs deletes the oldest logs above it.
LOGS_MAX_DISK_BYTES=5368709120
# VictoriaMetrics has no byte cap. It stops ingesting when free space on the Docker data
# filesystem falls to this reserve. The default, 8 GiB, is MIN_FREE_DISK_GIB (5) plus the 3 GiB an
# update needs at its peak, so metrics can never fill the disk to where ./chronicle update refuses.
# Raise it with MIN_FREE_DISK_GIB; check warns when it is lower. Update moves the old 2 GiB default.
METRICS_MIN_FREE_DISK_BYTES=8589934592
FLUENT_BIT_FORWARD_PORT=24224
CADVISOR_MEM_LIMIT=256m
VICTORIAMETRICS_MEM_LIMIT=512m
VICTORIALOGS_MEM_LIMIT=512m
FLUENT_BIT_MEM_LIMIT=128m
OPERATIONAL_PROBE_MEM_LIMIT=192m
PUBLIC_PROBE_MEM_LIMIT=64m
# The probe checks https://DOMAIN/health unless this names another public health URL.
# MONITORING_PUBLIC_HEALTH_URL=
# CA certificate the probe trusts for that URL when the public certificate comes from a
# private CA. Place it in the state tls/ directory and give its in-container path.
# MONITORING_PUBLIC_CA_FILE=/tls/public-ca.crt
# Seconds between operational probes (1-300).
# MONITORING_PROBE_INTERVAL_SECONDS=30
# Webhook that receives Grafana alerts (JSON POST). Unset keeps alerts in Grafana only.
# Point it at an internal relay (chat, pager, or mail gateway); see docs/MONITORING-RUNBOOK.md.
# A host other than loopback is the one thing that lets Grafana open connections off this host;
# ./chronicle decides that from this value on every run (docs/NETWORK-EGRESS.md).
# CHRONICLE_ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=
# Derived from the URL above, not a setting: open when it names a host other than loopback, else
# closed. Setup, ./chronicle update and every ./chronicle run write it here, rewriting a stale
# value with a warning, so a plain `docker compose` gives Grafana the same networks.
# CHRONICLE_ALERT_EGRESS=closed
METRICS_EXPORTER_MEM_LIMIT=64m
GRAFANA_MEM_LIMIT=512m

# Controlled-register reference to an approved backup storage encryption waiver, if needed.
#BACKUP_STORAGE_ENCRYPTION_WAIVER=

# Bound each scheduled dump plus scratch restore; overall startup must cover this work.
BACKUP_VERIFY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=3600

# First-party sidecars. Setup builds these from this source revision. Releases pin digests.
BACKUP_IMAGE=__CHRONICLE_BACKUP_IMAGE__
GRAFANA_IMAGE=__CHRONICLE_GRAFANA_IMAGE__
FLUENT_BIT_IMAGE=__CHRONICLE_SELFHOST_FLUENT_BIT_IMAGE__

# Optional previous dashboard signing key, written by routine jwt rotation. Never export it.
JWT_SECRET_PREVIOUS=
# Absolute Unix seconds. The verifier stops accepting the previous key at this instant,
# even if the old value remains in this protected file. Empty disables overlap.
JWT_SECRET_PREVIOUS_EXPIRES_AT=0

# Unset operator/test overrides (process environment only; do not add active assignments):
# CHRONICLE_RELEASES_URL= # replacement HTTP(S) release catalog, tried before GitHub; signatures remain mandatory.
# CHRONICLE_VERIFY_MOBILE_CANARY= # private (0600) JSON of synthetic mobile requests; release smoke only.
# CHRONICLE_MEMINFO= # alternate Linux-format meminfo input for host sizing checks.
# CADDY_SETUP_HASH_IMAGE= # Caddy password-hash helper image; receives the password on stdin.

# Container/CLI diagnostic inputs. These are supplied by wrapper/image defaults, not
# forwarded from this deployment .env; assignments remain commented and unset here.
# BACKUP_DIR=
# CHRONICLE_ALERT_SYSLOG=
# CHRONICLE_GUARD_TLS_DIR=
# CHRONICLE_RECONCILE_RUN=
# CHRONICLE_RESTORE_ORCHESTRATED=
# CHRONICLE_RESTORE_ROLLBACK=
# CHRONICLE_RESTORE_TRUSTED_SHA256=
# INTERNAL_HEALTH_URL=
# LOGS_HEALTH_URL=
# POSTGRES_MIGRATION_JDBC_URL=
# POSTGRES_MIGRATION_USER=
# POSTGRES_SSL_MODE=
# TLS_DIR=
# Optional process override: archive downloads and image pulls; default 600 seconds.
# CHRONICLE_TRANSFER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=

# Container/CLI diagnostic inputs supplied by wrappers, images or defaults; not forwarded from deployment .env. Keep assignments commented and unset.
# BACKUPS_DIR=
# OPERATION_STARTED_SECONDS=
# POSTGRES_MIGRATION_PASSWORD_FILE=
# POSTGRES_ROTATION_KEY=
# POSTGRES_ROTATION_ROLE=
